---
import { renderToString } from "katex";
import EvidenceDetails from "./EvidenceDetails.astro";
import type { StageView } from "../lib/site-data";
interface Props { view: StageView }
const { view } = Astro.props;
const bridge = view.interludes.find((item) => item.id === "graves-window-comparison");
if (bridge === undefined) throw new Error("Bahdanau needs its source-scoped window comparison.");
const evidence = [...new Map(bridge.blocks.flatMap((b) => b.evidence).map((e) => [e.claimId, e])).values()];
const math = (s: string) => renderToString(s, { displayMode: true, output: "htmlAndMathml", throwOnError: true });
---
<section class="reading-block alignment-prerequisite" id={bridge.id} aria-labelledby="graves-window-heading">
  <p class="kicker">Prerequisite comparison · handwriting and translation</p><h2 id="graves-window-heading">What does a forward-moving window permit?</h2>
  <p>Graves’s handwriting model reads a supplied character string while predicting a pen trace. Its lowest recurrent layer predicts positive amplitudes, width parameters and location increments for a mixture of ten Gaussian windows. Every center moves forward. The character-weighted sum is unnormalized; it is not a probability distribution.</p>
  <p class="mt-3">Bahdanau’s first manuscript names this as a similar approach and identifies the monotonically increasing locations as a limitation for translation, which may need source reordering. Its source-position softmax has no corresponding forward-only center constraint. This named comparison does not establish that the translator was derived from the handwriting system.</p>
  {view.mode === "expert" && <><div class="equation-panel" role="region" aria-label="Graves v5 soft-window equations 46 through 51" tabindex="0"><div set:html={math(String.raw`\phi(t,u)=\sum_{k=1}^{K}\alpha_t^k\exp\!\left[-\beta_t^k(\kappa_t^k-u)^2\right],\quad w_t=\sum_{u=1}^{U}\phi(t,u)c_u`)} /><div set:html={math(String.raw`\alpha_t=\exp(\widehat\alpha_t),\quad\beta_t=\exp(\widehat\beta_t),\quad\kappa_t=\kappa_{t-1}+\exp(\widehat\kappa_t)`)} /></div><p>Graves v5 §5.1, article p. 26, Eqs. 46–51. Here cᵤ is a one-hot character vector and α is a Gaussian amplitude; neither symbol has Bahdanau’s meaning. The window enters upper layers at t and the first layer at t + 1 to avoid a cycle. The inspected text leaves κ₀ unspecified.</p></>}
  <details class="prediction-reveal mt-4"><summary>Check window movement and normalization</summary><p>Each Gaussian center must move forward, although overlapping windows can still weight nearby earlier characters. This is a constraint on the centers, not a claim that every character weight or the mixture’s maximum moves monotonically. Bahdanau’s read can emphasize earlier source positions again. Only the translation softmax here guarantees source weights summing to one.</p></details>
  <details class="mt-4"><summary>Keep Graves’s window revisions separate</summary><p class="mt-3">The window exists in v1. The derivative formulas change later: v3 retains the earlier Eqs. 56–60; v4 corrects component indexing and Gaussian factors; v5 puts the amplitude inside ε and removes its duplicate external factor in the next equation. The displayed forward operations follow v5. Later derivative corrections do not move back into v1, and this comparison calculates no handwriting output or translation window.</p></details>
  <p class="mt-3"><a href={`/journeys/encoder-decoder-alignment/${view.mode}/sutskever-2014/#graves-formulation-prerequisite`}>Revisit the separate Graves LSTM formulation in {view.mode} mode</a></p><a class="claim-link" href="/reference/works/graves-2013-generating-sequences-recurrent-neural-networks/">Inspect Graves’s complete source record</a>
  {evidence.map((item) => <EvidenceDetails evidence={item} />)}
</section>
